# Level06

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/ptrace.h>

int auth(char *login, unsigned int serial)
{
    unsigned int check;
    int len;
    int i;

    login[strcspn(login, "\n")] = 0;
    len = strnlen(login, 32);
    if (len <= 5)
        return 1;
    if (ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME, 0, 1, 0) == -1)
    {
        puts("| !! TAMPERING DETECTED !!  |");
        return 1;
    }
    check = ((signed char)login[3] ^ 0x1337) + 0x5eeded;
    for (i = 0; i < len; i++)
    {
        if ((signed char)login[i] <= 0x1f)
            return 1;
        check += ((signed char)login[i] ^ check) % 1337;
    }
    if (serial == check)
        return 0;
    return 1;
}

int main(void)
{
    char login[32];
    unsigned int serial;

    printf("-> Enter Login: ");
    fgets(login, 32, stdin);
    printf("-> Enter Serial: ");
    scanf("%u", &serial);
    if (auth(login, serial) == 0)
    {
        puts("Authenticated!");
        system("/bin/sh");
    }
    return (0);
}
```

## Vulnerability

There is no memory corruption here; the level is a keygen puzzle. The program
reads a login (text) and a serial (unsigned integer), then calls
`auth(login, serial)`. If `auth` returns `0`, it spawns a shell with
`system("/bin/sh")`. The binary is setuid `level07`, so that shell runs as
level07.

`auth` turns the login into a number `check` and succeeds only if
`serial == check`:

1. The login must be longer than 5 characters, and every character must be
   printable (`> 0x1f`).
2. `ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME, ...)` is an anti-debug guard: it returns `-1` only
   when the process is already traced (running under `gdb`), which prints
   "TAMPERING DETECTED" and fails. Run normally, it succeeds, so there is
   nothing to bypass as long as the program is not launched under a debugger.
3. `check` starts from `((signed char)login[3] ^ 0x1337) + 0x5eeded`, then for
   each character does `check += (login[i] ^ check) % 1337`.

The weakness: this computation uses nothing secret and nothing random. It can be
replayed offline. We pick any valid login, compute its `check` with the exact
same formula, and feed that value back as the serial.

In decimal the constants are: `0x1337 = 4919`, `0x5eeded = 6221293`,
`0x1f = 31`.

## Exploitation

The serial is computed by a small keygen that copies the loop of `auth`
(`Ressources/keygen.c`): same starting value, same `% 1337`, without the
`ptrace`, length and comparison checks.

```sh
gcc -o keygen Ressources/keygen.c
./keygen aaaaaa        # serial: 6231562
```

The login `aaaaaa` (6 characters) passes the `len > 5` check; its serial is
`6231562`. Feed both to the program:

```sh
(python -c 'print "aaaaaa\n6231562"'; cat) | ./level06
whoami                              # level07
cat /home/users/level07/.pass
```

`auth` returns `0` -> `Authenticated!` -> `system("/bin/sh")` gives a shell as
level07. The `; cat` keeps stdin open for that shell.

Any login of 6+ printable characters works; just regenerate its serial with the
keygen (e.g. `./keygen azerty` -> `6232813`).

